// X64PrologueEpilogue — inserts frame setup/teardown ops after register
// allocation. EVERY function gets a REAL FRAME POINTER: the prologue opens with
// `push rbp` / `mov rbp, rsp`, then pushes the callee-saved registers the coloring
// used, then reserves the frame with `sub rsp, N`; the epilogue mirrors all three
// before each `ret`. This pass owns ALL of it — unlike v1, where ISel pre-emits an
// `epilogue` at each return and this pass strips/re-emits it. shv2's return
// lowering emits only the R8 move + `ret`.
//
// ⭐ WHY A FRAME POINTER, AND WHY ON EVERY FUNCTION INCLUDING LEAVES
//
// The frame pointer's job is not addressing — every local and spill slot here is
// addressed off `rsp` (see `spillSlotBaseDisp`), and that does not change. Its job is
// the SAVED-RBP CHAIN: `[rbp]` is the caller's rbp and `[rbp+8]` is the return address,
// so the frames of a live thread form a linked list that can be WALKED. Two things need
// exactly that list, and neither can be served by anything cheaper:
//
//   1. The PANIC BACKTRACE (P1.0d.3, here). `mrt_panic_walk_frames` follows the chain to
//      symbolize the callers of a fault or a panic. Frame 0 comes from the faulting RIP,
//      but every frame above it comes from the chain.
//   2. GROWABLE GREEN-THREAD STACKS (R3 @ P1.5, PLAN.md). A Go-style `morestack` allocates
//      a larger stack, COPIES the old one onto it, and must then find and fix every
//      interior frame pointer — which have all just moved. The chain is HOW it finds them:
//      v1's `__gt_morestack` walks it and adds the relocation offset to each saved rbp
//      (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Targets/X64/X64Backend.maxon:7094` — "fixes the
//      saved-RBP chain + the entry RSP by the relocation offset"; step 6b at :7172 is the
//      walk itself). A relocating stack without a frame-pointer chain has no way to
//      enumerate the pointers it just invalidated.
//
// So shv2 needs this at P1.5 regardless of stack traces, and building it now costs the
// committed goldens ONCE instead of moving them again later on top of a shipped runtime.
//
// LEAVES ARE INCLUDED, and that is the point rather than an oversight: a frameless leaf
// cannot be unwound OUT of, so it terminates any walk that reaches it — and the faulting
// `main` of `specs-shv2/safety.md` IS a frameless leaf. A chain with a hole in it is not a
// chain. There is therefore no `needsFrame` test any more: the frame POINTER is
// unconditional, while the frame RESERVATION (`sub rsp, N`) is still elided when there is
// nothing to reserve.
//
// It costs NO register. `allocatablePool` already excludes rsp/rbp ("the frame") and
// always has, so shv2 was paying a frame pointer's full price — 14 allocatable GPRs, not
// 15 — while leaving rbp holding whatever the loader put there. The pool is unchanged;
// this is the same 14 registers, now with the benefit collected.
//
// (This REPLACES the frame-pointer-omitted design this file used to describe. That choice
// predated the morestack requirement above; see ARCHITECTURE.md's backend section.)
//
// The runtime entry stub `mrt_start` builds its own frame directly in
// `augmentWithRuntime` (which runs AFTER this pass), so this pass never sees it — it
// sets up the same `push rbp` / `mov rbp, rsp` by hand, because the chain has to start
// somewhere and a trace that cannot name `mrt_start` stops one frame short. Stack
// arguments (a 7th+ parameter) and real spill slots grow the frame further at their
// milestones; today `outgoingArgSize` is 0 and `stackSize` is 0 for every function.

// x64 frames keep rsp 16-byte aligned at each call site. Exported because the panic
// runtime lays its own frames by hand (X64Runtime) and must land on the SAME residue: a
// second `= 16` over there would be this fact written down twice, and the two could only
// ever disagree silently — a misaligned rsp is not a compile error, it is a crash inside
// kernel32's SSE, arbitrarily far from whichever copy was wrong.
export let StackFrameAlignment = 16

// `push rbp` is a push like any other, and the frame arithmetic is the one place that has
// to know it: it moves rsp by 8 exactly as a callee-saved push does, so it counts toward
// the parity that decides how much `sub rsp` must reserve to land 16-aligned AT the call.
// Named rather than folded into a `+ 1`, because a bare 1 in that expression is exactly
// the magic value that makes an alignment bug invisible.
let FramePointerPushCount = 1 as RegCount

// Win64 requires a caller to reserve 32 bytes of SHADOW SPACE below its outgoing args
// — scratch the callee may use for its first four register parameters. Every function
// that makes a call reserves it, `mrt_start`'s hand-built 0x20 frame included.
export let ShadowSpaceBytes = 32 as StackSize

// The custom-ABI callee-saved GPRs, in PUSH order (popped in reverse). The register
// allocator may color values into any of these; the prologue pushes exactly the ones a
// function's coloring used, and the epilogue pops them. rbx + r12–r15 mirror
// RegisterAllocator.calleeSavedMask (kept in one order here so push/pop stay paired).
// EXPORTED so the GT context switch (X64GtRuntime) saves EXACTLY the set the prologue
// pushes — one ordered list, not a second copy free to drift into register corruption
// across a switch.
export let calleeSavedOrder = [X64Register.rbx, X64Register.r12, X64Register.r13, X64Register.r14, X64Register.r15]

typealias X64RegisterArray = Array with X64Register

// Insert prologue/epilogue ops for every function in the module.
export function insertPrologueEpilogue(module TargetModule, target Target)
	logDebug(LogCategory.codegen, message: function() gives "Prologue/epilogue insertion ({targetToString(target)}): {module.funcCount()} function(s)")
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		insertFrameForFunction(module, func: func)
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'insertPrologueEpilogue'

// Insert a function's frame: establish the frame pointer, push the callee-saved registers
// its coloring used, reserve its frame (`sub rsp`), and mirror all three on every return.
//
// EVERY function is framed, leaves included — the saved-RBP chain is only a chain if it has
// no holes (see this file's header). The frame RESERVATION is still conditional: a leaf that
// reserves nothing emits no `sub rsp`, since `computeFrameBytes` returns 0 and both
// `encodePrologue` and `encodeEpilogue` skip a zero-size frame.
//
// `usedCalleeSavedRegs` is read BEFORE anything is inserted, so it reports what the COLORING
// used and never sees this pass's own `push rbp`.
function insertFrameForFunction(module TargetModule, func IrFunction)
	let usedCallee = usedCalleeSavedRegs(module, func: func)
	let makesCall = functionMakesCall(module, func: func)

	// The SAME `computeFrameBytes` the GT stack guard reaches through `computeGuardFrameBytes` — one formula, two
	// callers, so the guard's projected rsp and this `sub rsp, N` can never disagree.
	let frameBytes = computeFrameBytes(func, makesCall: makesCall, pushCount: usedCallee.count())

	var isFirstBlock = true
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		var block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("insertPrologueEpilogue: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

		if isFirstBlock 'entry'
			insertPrologueAtEntry(module, block: block, usedCallee: usedCallee, frameBytes: frameBytes)
		end 'entry'
		isFirstBlock = false

		if blockEndsWithReturn(module, block: block) 'returnBlock'
			appendEpilogueBeforeReturn(module, block: block, usedCallee: usedCallee, frameBytes: frameBytes)
		end 'returnBlock'
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'insertFrameForFunction'

// The frame this function's prologue reserves with `sub rsp, N` — the SINGLE source of that number, so the
// prologue pass and the GT stack guard (X64Backend's `emitFunctionChunk`, which projects rsp by exactly this
// much) cannot derive two different frame sizes for one function. It is the `computeFrameBytes` expression
// `insertFrameForFunction` used inline, lifted here and exported because the guard needs the same answer.
export function computeGuardFrameBytes(module TargetModule, func IrFunction) returns StackSize
	let makesCall = functionMakesCall(module, func: func)
	let pushCount = usedCalleeSavedRegs(module, func: func).count()
	return computeFrameBytes(func, makesCall: makesCall, pushCount: pushCount)
end 'computeGuardFrameBytes'

// The bytes to reserve with `sub rsp, N`.
//   • A CALLER must leave rsp 16-byte aligned AT the call. Entry rsp ≡ 8 (mod 16) — the
//     `call` that reached us pushed the return address. Every push then moves it by 8, and
//     the `sub` must land it on ≡ 0. The pushes are `push rbp` PLUS the `pushCount`
//     callee-saved ones, which is the whole reason the frame pointer appears in this
//     arithmetic: it is one more push, so it INVERTS the parity the reservation must
//     correct for. It must also cover shadow space + locals + any stack-passed args (0 at
//     M5). `mrt_start`'s hand-built frame IS this formula with pushCount 0, stackSize 0.
//   • A LEAF has no call, so alignment is irrelevant; it reserves just its (16-rounded)
//     locals — 0 for the leaves that have none. push/pop alone balance rsp.
function computeFrameBytes(func IrFunction, makesCall bool, pushCount RegCount) returns StackSize
	if not makesCall 'leaf'
		return alignUp(func.stackSize, alignment: StackFrameAlignment)
	end 'leaf'
	let base = func.stackSize + func.outgoingArgSize + ShadowSpaceBytes
	let targetMod = frameAlignTarget(pushCount + FramePointerPushCount)
	return roundUpToMod(base, targetMod: targetMod, modulus: StackFrameAlignment)
end 'computeFrameBytes'

// The rsp offset of a function's spill-slot AREA — the base the x64 encoder adds to
// `slot * SpillSlotBytes` for every spill/reload. The frame the prologue reserves is laid
// out, from rsp upward: [shadow space + outgoing args] [spill slots] [alignment padding].
// So spill slots must sit ABOVE the shadow space on a CALLER: at a `call`, the callee may
// clobber the caller's 32-byte shadow space (`[rsp .. rsp+32)`), which would corrupt a
// value spilled across a loop whose body makes a call. Placing the slot area at
// `ShadowSpaceBytes + outgoingArgSize` keeps every slot out of that clobber zone. A LEAF
// (no call) reserves no shadow space, so its slots start at rsp (base 0) — the simplest
// form, matching the design's "slot disp = slotIndex*8 from rsp". This is the SINGLE place
// the shadow-space-vs-slot ordering is decided; `computeFrameBytes` reserves exactly enough
// (`stackSize + outgoingArgSize + ShadowSpaceBytes`) for a caller to hold both.
export function spillSlotBaseDisp(module TargetModule, func IrFunction) returns StackSize
	if functionMakesCall(module, func: func) 'caller'
		return ShadowSpaceBytes + func.outgoingArgSize
	end 'caller'
	return 0
end 'spillSlotBaseDisp'

// The `sub rsp` size's required residue mod 16 for a caller with `pushCount` TOTAL pushes
// (the frame pointer's own included — see `computeFrameBytes`): 8 when even (entry rsp ≡ 8
// stays ≡ 8 after an even number of pushes, so an 8 (mod 16) sub reaches ≡ 0), 0 when odd.
function frameAlignTarget(pushCount RegCount) returns StackSize
	if pushCount mod 2 == 0 'even'
		return 8
	end 'even'
	return 0
end 'frameAlignTarget'

// The smallest value >= `base` congruent to `targetMod` modulo `modulus` — `base` plus
// the least non-negative delta that lands its residue on `targetMod`.
function roundUpToMod(base StackSize, targetMod StackSize, modulus StackSize) returns StackSize
	// `modulus` is a power-of-two stack alignment, always non-zero; cast so the two remainders are
	// provably safe (integer `mod` throws for a possibly-zero divisor).
	let m = modulus as NonZeroWord
	let delta = ((targetMod - (base mod m)) + m) mod m
	return base + delta
end 'roundUpToMod'

// The callee-saved registers a function's colored body references, in push order. The
// allocator colors values into rbx/r12–r15 for values live across a call (or under
// pressure); each such register must be preserved, so it is pushed/popped. Scanned by
// intersecting every op's physical registers with the callee-saved set.
function usedCalleeSavedRegs(module TargetModule, func IrFunction) returns X64RegisterArray
	var mask = 0 as RegMask
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("usedCalleeSavedRegs: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			mask = mask or opPhysicalRegs(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("usedCalleeSavedRegs: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops"))
		end 'eachOp'
		if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'hasTerm'
			mask = mask or opPhysicalRegs(try module.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("usedCalleeSavedRegs: terminator not in module.ops"))
		end 'hasTerm'
	end 'eachBlock'

	var used = X64RegisterArray.create()
	for reg in calleeSavedOrder 'eachCallee'
		if regMaskContains(mask, regNum: reg.rawValue) 'isUsed'
			used.push(reg)
		end 'isUsed'
	end 'eachCallee'
	return used
end 'usedCalleeSavedRegs'

// Every physical GPR a colored TargetOp names, as a mask. Runs on the fully-colored
// program (post-regalloc, post-SSA-destruction — `xchgRegReg` may be present, so this
// does NOT route through `targetOpOperands`, which rejects that post-coloring op). Ops
// with no register operands contribute nothing. EXHAUSTIVE over TargetOp — a new op
// fails to compile here until its registers are declared.
function opPhysicalRegs(op TargetOp) returns RegMask
	return match op 'p'
		prologue to jcc gives 0
		movRegImm(dest, _) gives physRegBit(dest)
		movRegImm32(dest, _) gives physRegBit(dest)
		movRegReg(d, s) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(s)
		movReg32Reg32(d, s) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(s)
		subRegReg(d, l, r) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l) or physRegBit(r)
		imulRegReg(d, l, r) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l) or physRegBit(r)
		negReg(d, s) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(s)
		cmpRegReg(l, r) gives physRegBit(l) or physRegBit(r)
		leaRegRegReg(d, b, i) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(b) or physRegBit(i)
		leaRegRegImm32(d, b, _) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(b)
		imulRegRegImm32(d, s, _) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(s)
		cmpRegImm32(reg, _) gives physRegBit(reg)
		xchgRegReg(a, b) gives physRegBit(a) or physRegBit(b)
		callDirect to cqo gives 0
		idivReg(divisor) gives physRegBit(divisor)
		pushReg(reg) gives regMaskOf(reg)
		popReg(reg) gives regMaskOf(reg)
		storeSlotReg(_, srcReg) gives physRegBit(srcReg)
		loadRegSlot(destReg, _) gives physRegBit(destReg)
		andRegReg(d, l, r) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l) or physRegBit(r)
		orRegReg(d, l, r) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l) or physRegBit(r)
		xorRegReg(d, l, r) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l) or physRegBit(r)
		notReg(d, s) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(s)
		andRegImm32(d, l, _) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l)
		orRegImm32(d, l, _) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l)
		xorRegImm32(d, l, _) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l)
		shlRegImm8(d, l, _) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l)
		sarRegImm8(d, l, _) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l)
		shrRegImm8(d, l, _) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l)
		// RCX is NOT reported here. This function answers "which registers did the COLORING
		// use?", so the prologue knows which callee-saved ones to preserve — and RCX is
		// caller-saved, so it needs no preservation regardless. The shift's use of CL is an
		// IMPLICIT one (a `TargetOpMeta.implicitUses` mask), not a coloring outcome.
		shlRegCl(d, l) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l)
		sarRegCl(d, l) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l)
		shrRegCl(d, l) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l)
		setccReg(d, _) gives physRegBit(d)
		// ⚠ The data-section band MUST be counted here, and this is the arm where a miss is a real
		// miscompile rather than a missed optimization: this mask is what decides which CALLEE-SAVED
		// registers the prologue pushes. A global's address, or the value loaded out of one, can be
		// colored into rbx or r12-r15 like any other value — and if that register is not in this mask,
		// the prologue never saves it and the function silently clobbers its CALLER's register.
		leaRegGlobal(d, _) gives physRegBit(d)
		loadRegBaseDisp(d, b, _, _) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(b)
		storeBaseDispReg(b, _, s, _) gives physRegBit(b) or physRegBit(s)
		// The SSE band. Reported exactly like every other op, and DELIBERATELY not special-cased
		// for Wave 1: every XMM is caller-saved right now, so intersecting this mask with the
		// callee-saved set yields nothing and no XMM is ever pushed. Answering honestly here
		// rather than short-circuiting on "floats never need saving" is what makes Wave 2 — where
		// xmm6-15 BECOME callee-saved — a change to the callee-saved mask alone, instead of a
		// change to the mask plus a hunt for every place that assumed otherwise.
		addsdRegReg(d, l, r) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l) or physRegBit(r)
		subsdRegReg(d, l, r) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l) or physRegBit(r)
		mulsdRegReg(d, l, r) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l) or physRegBit(r)
		divsdRegReg(d, l, r) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(l) or physRegBit(r)
		ucomisdRegReg(l, r) gives physRegBit(l) or physRegBit(r)
		movsdRegRip(d, _) gives physRegBit(d)
		cvtsi2sdRegReg(d, s) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(s)
		cvttsd2siRegReg(d, s) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(s)
		// The MATH INTRINSICS — reported like every other SSE op, for the reason the band comment
		// above gives: no XMM is callee-saved this wave, so this contributes nothing today, and
		// answering honestly is what makes Wave 2 a change to the callee-saved mask alone.
		sqrtsdRegReg(d, s) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(s)
		roundsdRegReg(d, s, _) gives physRegBit(d) or physRegBit(s)
		// The `.rdata` address `lea` (P1.2 wave A) — its GPR dest can be colored into a callee-saved
		// register, so it MUST be counted here, exactly like `leaRegGlobal` above.
		leaRegRdata(d, _) gives physRegBit(d)
		// arm64 does not run the x64 prologue pass (its frame is `Arm64PrologueEpilogue`, wave 1), so
		// no arm64 op reaches here — an empty mask is unreachable AND correct (none of these arms names
		// a register the x64 prologue counts). `gives` cannot host a `panic`; the band-append
		// invariant forced this arm.
		arm64Ret to arm64Syscall gives 0
		// ⚠ The P1.5-A1 function-value band MUST be counted, and this is another arm where a miss is a
		// real miscompile, not a missed optimization: BOTH name a GPR the coloring can place in a
		// callee-saved register (rbx/r12-r15) — `leaRegFunc`'s dest and `callReg`'s callee-address use —
		// so if either is absent from this mask the prologue never saves that register and the function
		// silently clobbers its CALLER's. The same trap the `leaRegGlobal`/`leaRegRdata` arms document.
		leaRegFunc(d, _) gives physRegBit(d)
		callReg(r) gives physRegBit(r)
		// The Linux `syscall` names no explicit register operand (its number is an immediate, its args
		// are separate `mov`s, and rax/rcx/r11 are caller-saved and never pushed) — 0, like `callDirect`/
		// `iatCall` above.
		x64Syscall gives 0
		// The x64 jump table's biased index USE can be colored into a callee-saved register, so it MUST be
		// counted (the same real-miscompile trap `callReg` documents). The scratch r10/r11 (its implicitDefs)
		// are caller-saved and never pushed, so they contribute nothing. `arm64JumpTable` never runs the x64
		// prologue pass, so 0 (its own prologue counts it) — but the arm keeps the match exhaustive.
		x64JumpTable(indexReg, _, _, _) gives physRegBit(indexReg)
		arm64JumpTable gives 0
		// The witness call's base-register USE can be colored into a callee-saved register, so it MUST be
		// counted — the same trap `callReg` documents.
		callMem(baseReg, _) gives physRegBit(baseReg)
	end 'p'
end 'opPhysicalRegs'

// The single-register mask of a colored operand. Every operand is physical at this
// point (register allocation rewrote virtuals), so a virtual here is an allocator bug.
function physRegBit(vreg TargetVReg) returns RegMask
	match vreg 'v'
		physical(reg) then return regMaskAdd(0, regNum: reg)
		virtual(id) then panic("opPhysicalRegs: unresolved virtual register v{id} reached the prologue pass — register allocation must color every operand first")
	end 'v'
end 'physRegBit'

// Prepend the frame-pointer setup, then the callee-saved pushes, then the frame `sub` to
// the entry block, so at runtime they run in that order. Built by inserting at index 0 in
// REVERSE (`sub` first, then each push before it, then the frame pointer before them all),
// so the final order is: push rbp, mov rbp/rsp, push₀ … pushₙ, sub.
//
// The frame pointer is established FIRST, before any callee-saved push, and that ordering
// is what makes the chain well-formed: at `mov rbp, rsp` the only things below the return
// address are the caller's saved rbp, so `[rbp]` is the caller's frame pointer and
// `[rbp+8]` is the return address — the two loads `mrt_panic_walk_frames` does. Pushing a
// callee-saved register first would bury the return address one slot deeper per push, and
// the walker has no way to know how many.
function insertPrologueAtEntry(module TargetModule, block IrBlock, usedCallee X64RegisterArray, frameBytes StackSize)
	if frameBytes > 0 'reserve'
		prependOp(module, block: block, op: TargetOp.prologue(frameBytes))
	end 'reserve'
	for ri in 0 upto usedCallee.count() 'eachPushRev'
		let reg = try usedCallee.get(usedCallee.count() - 1 - ri) otherwise panic("insertPrologueAtEntry: usedCallee.get OOB")
		prependOp(module, block: block, op: TargetOp.pushReg(reg))
	end 'eachPushRev'
	prependOp(module, block: block, op: framePointerSetupOp())
	prependOp(module, block: block, op: TargetOp.pushReg(X64Register.rbp))
end 'insertPrologueAtEntry'

// Append the frame teardown before the `ret` terminator, mirroring the prologue exactly in
// reverse: release the frame (`add rsp`), pop the callee-saved registers in REVERSE push
// order (LIFO), then restore the caller's frame pointer. Appended to the body, which runs
// before the `ret` terminator.
//
// `pop rbp` is reached with rsp back at the value `mov rbp, rsp` captured, because the
// `add rsp` and the pops undo the `sub` and the pushes one for one. (v1 and the C#
// reference instead spell the epilogue `mov rsp, rbp; pop rbp`, which restores rsp from the
// frame pointer rather than by balanced arithmetic — but that form CANNOT also restore the
// callee-saved registers, which sit below rbp and would be skipped over. shv2 pushes them
// after the frame pointer, so it unwinds them explicitly and the balance is what carries
// rsp home.)
function appendEpilogueBeforeReturn(module TargetModule, block IrBlock, usedCallee X64RegisterArray, frameBytes StackSize)
	if frameBytes > 0 'release'
		module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.epilogue(frameBytes))
	end 'release'
	for ri in 0 upto usedCallee.count() 'eachPopRev'
		let reg = try usedCallee.get(usedCallee.count() - 1 - ri) otherwise panic("appendEpilogueBeforeReturn: usedCallee.get OOB")
		module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.popReg(reg))
	end 'eachPopRev'
	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.popReg(X64Register.rbp))
end 'appendEpilogueBeforeReturn'

// `mov rbp, rsp` — the instruction that makes rbp name this frame. Reuses the existing
// 64-bit reg-reg move rather than minting a TargetOp variant for it: the bytes are an
// ordinary `mov r/m64, r64`, and a new variant would have to be band-appended for no
// encoding it does not already have. Shared by this pass and `mrt_start`'s hand-built
// frame, so the two cannot spell the frame's defining instruction differently.
export function framePointerSetupOp() returns TargetOp
	return TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rbp), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp))
end 'framePointerSetupOp'

// Insert `op` at the front of `block`'s op list (a fresh `module.ops` slot). The shared
// "prepend a new op" helper the prologue's pushes and `sub` both use.
function prependOp(module TargetModule, block IrBlock, op TargetOp)
	let opIdx = module.ops.count()
	module.ops.push(op)
	insertOpRef(block, at: 0, opIdx: opIdx)
end 'prependOp'

// True when the function makes a call — the frame trigger that forces 16-byte alignment
// + shadow space even for a leaf-caller with no locals. `isCall` is read straight off
// each op's TargetOpMeta backing.
function functionMakesCall(module TargetModule, func IrFunction) returns bool
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("functionMakesCall: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			if opAtIsCall(module, opIdx: opIdx) 'call'
				return true
			end 'call'
		end 'eachOp'

		if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'hasTerm'
			if opAtIsCall(module, opIdx: block.terminatorIndex()) 'callTerm'
				return true
			end 'callTerm'
		end 'hasTerm'
	end 'eachBlock'

	return false
end 'functionMakesCall'

function opAtIsCall(module TargetModule, opIdx OpIndex) returns bool
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("functionMakesCall: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops")
	return op.rawValue.isCall
end 'opAtIsCall'

// True when the block's terminator is a `ret` — the site an `epilogue` precedes.
function blockEndsWithReturn(module TargetModule, block IrBlock) returns bool
	if not block.hasTerminatorOp() 'noTerm'
		return false
	end 'noTerm'
	let op = try module.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("blockEndsWithReturn: terminator not in module.ops")
	return match op 'check'
		prologue to epilogue gives false
		ret gives true
		jmp to idivReg gives false
		pushReg to popReg gives false
		storeSlotReg to loadRegSlot gives false
		andRegReg to leaRegRdata gives false
		// Unreachable this rung (arm64 uses Arm64PrologueEpilogue, wave 1). `gives` cannot host a
		// `panic`; wave 1 answers `arm64Ret` true here when it wires the arm64 return block.
		arm64Ret to arm64Syscall gives false
		// `leaRegFunc`/`callReg` (P1.5-A1) and the Linux `x64Syscall` are body ops, never a `ret`.
		leaRegFunc to x64Syscall gives false
		// The jump tables are branch terminators, not a `ret`.
		x64JumpTable to arm64JumpTable gives false
		// The witness call is a body op, never a `ret`.
		callMem gives false
	end 'check'
end 'blockEndsWithReturn'
